            | chromaticity coordinates of the red, green, and blue primaries can be obtained if the matrix is given.Also, if the
            i(xmlns:doc='urn://wiley-online-library/content/document', xmlns:d='urn://wiley-online-library/content/document') x
            | ,
            i(xmlns:doc='urn://wiley-online-library/content/document', xmlns:d='urn://wiley-online-library/content/document') y
            | or
            i(xmlns:doc='urn://wiley-online-library/content/document', xmlns:d='urn://wiley-online-library/content/document') u?,v?
            | chromaticity coordinates of the three primaries and a postulated white point are given, the matrixcoefficients can be determined, which is the foundation for modeling the optimum solution to theyellow?blue and purple problems as an optimization problem. In the section ?Possible primaries andcoordinates for the modified CAT02 matrix,? the constraints for the chromaticity coordinates of theprimaries and matrix satisfying the nesting rule are studied. In the section ?Fitting the visualexperimental data,? a two?variable objective function involving visual experimental datasets is proposedfor optimization. In the section ?The optimum solution,? MATLAB is used to solve the constrainednon?linear optimization problem and an optimum solution (that is, a new CAT02 matrix) is found. Finally,the section ?Conclusions? contains the main conclusions.
